ence","identifier":"doc:\/\/src.KeyboardKit\/documentation\/KeyboardKit\/SwiftUICore\/Color\/keyboardBackground(for:)","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":". These colors vary the color result based on the context. Prefer these context-based colors whenever possible."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"text":"Icons","type":"heading","anchor":"Icons","level":3},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"KeyboardKit defines variable-based icons like "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/src.KeyboardKit\/documentation\/KeyboardKit\/SwiftUICore\/Image\/keyboardNewline","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":", which make it easy to use the same icons as the framework does."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"name":"Important","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Some keyboard colors are semi-transparent to work around a system bug in iOS, where iOS defines an invalid color scheme when a keyboard is used with a dark appearance text field in light mode. iOS will say that the color scheme is "},{"code":".dark","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", even if the system color scheme is light. Since dark appearance keyboards in light mode look quite different from keyboards in dark mode, this makes it impossible to apply the correct style. This has been "},{"identifier":"https:\/\/github.com\/KeyboardKit\/KeyboardKit\/issues\/305","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":", but until it’s fixes, thse colors will stay semi-transparent."}]}],"style":"important","type":"aside"},{"text":"Fonts","anchor":"Fonts","level":3,"type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"KeyboardKit also defines a custom "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/src.KeyboardKit\/documentation\/KeyboardKit\/KeyboardFont","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" struct that can be used instead of the native "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/src.KeyboardKit\/documentation\/KeyboardKit\/SwiftUICore\/Font","isActive":true},{"type":"text",